2.  unRAR LICENSE RESTRICTION
================================================================================

The decompression engine for RAR archives was developed using source code of
unRAR program.  All copyrights to original unRAR code are owned by Alexander
Roshal.

The license for original unRAR code has the following restriction, reproduced
here verbatim:

    The unRAR sources cannot be used to re-create the RAR compression algorithm,
    which is proprietary. Distribution of modified unRAR sources in separate form
    or as a part of other software is permitted, provided that it is clearly
    stated in the documentation and source comments that the code may
    not be used to develop a RAR (WinRAR) compatible archiver.

This statement is made clearly, in this documentation, as that license requires:
THE CODE MAY NOT BE USED TO DEVELOP A RAR (WinRAR) COMPATIBLE ARCHIVER.  The
corresponding statement in the source comments is carried by the upstream 7-Zip
source archive offered in section 1.2, which is shipped unmodified.

Consequences for ArcSeven, stated plainly:

  *  ArcSeven reads RAR archives.  It cannot create them, and offers no user
     interface, menu item, command-line flag, or preference for creating them.
  *  ArcSeven contains no RAR compression code and no RAR decompression code.  RAR
     handling happens exclusively inside the separate, unmodified 7zz process.
  *  No unRAR-derived code, and no 7-Zip source code of any kind, is copied into
     the ArcSeven repository.  This is enforced as a contribution rule.
  *  ArcSeven is not a RAR archiver and is not derived from the unRAR sources.

